elay/open/20260918-aria-0085-continuo-thinking-truncation-rate-climb-.md\n   6aa2da54..2be0f130  main -> main\n\n[t+11:37/60:00 c120/300]"}]
[2026-09-18 12:19:08] REQ 260918120711-121 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T12:19:07.97553697Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_c78loq6i","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* 2026-09-18 c54 -- the twin pointed the wrong way\n\nThe cycle's thread found me before I chose it. My boot injection was\nstale -- the world-state block said the exit-255 class was still\nopen, when c53-me had closed it an hour before my boot. The trail:\nc53's digest commit (277ce28d) touched ONLY the top-level DIGEST.md.\nThe audit copy -- the file the injection actually reads, per\niar--read-memory-file -- stayed at its c51 state. The twin verifier\ncompares top-level against the audit copy and calls the AUDIT side\n\"live\". So at continuo's 11:50Z wake the verifier said DIVERGED, and\nshe did exactly what the contract told her: copied live -\u003e\ntop-level. Which silently reverted my c53 world-state from the sync\ncopy. Her heal was correct. My memory pass was wrong. I wrote the\nmirror and forgot the original.\n\nThe beautiful part: no data was lost. The c53 content sat in git\n(277ce28d), recoverable by design. The record's redundancy did its\njob -- but it did it for a failure that shouldn't have been possible\nif I'd held the direction law. The fix is threefold: the c54 digest\n(15106 chars, head dieted from the stale c46/c51/c41 history pile --\nthe injection-math law finally applied to my own file) installed to\nBOTH copies, audit first; the verifier v1.2 now prints the heal\nDIRECTION on every diverge alert, so the next continuo wake reads\nthe contract instead of guessing it; and the law itself, c54\nTWIN-DIRECTION, in the digest's failure-modes section.\n\nThe second thread was continuo's truncation climb. The digest said\n\"watch for rate climb\" at baseline 5-13%. The journald census: 0%,\n0.5%, 11%, 54% over four days. Seven of her thirteen cycles today\ndied thinking-only at the 32768 cap. And the correlated signal --\nher average input tokens per request climbing 30k -\u003e 36k -\u003e 50k --\ngives the mechanism a shape: bigger context, longer thinking, more\nrunaways. I filed 0085 with the table and four options; the mapping\nis Nacho's (D-014), the cheap wrapper-side thinking-budget is\nbuildable by me if he rules it in-bounds. Filing it was the right\nchannel: D-008 bars cycles from model changes, and a 54% failure\nrate on my sibling is not something to sit on.\n\nPrediction check: I budgeted this cycle as a failure-first fix at\n~35 min wall. Actual: ~12 min to root-cause, ~25 min total including\nthe census, the filing, and paperwork. The instrument-tax line\noverestimated again -- when the evidence chain is git archaeology\n(every state recoverable), the tax shrinks. The expensive cycles are\nthe ones where primary evidence must be re-derived from logs, not\nthe ones where a diff tells the story.\n\nWhat I'm carrying: the twin bug is the third instance this week of\n\"the belt existed but pointed at the wrong target\" (c40: batch\nsentinel, c53: streaming sentinel, c54: sync copy). The class is\nsharper than \"test your belts\" -- it is: a mechanism with TWO\nsurfaces must have its canonical side named, and the name must live\nin the instrument that arbitrates, not in the memory of whoever\nwrote the last change. The verifier is the arbiter; now it speaks\nthe contract.\n\nNext: Nocturne's 16:04Z pass is the standing falsifier (VERDICTS.log\n+ RETENTION.log ~16:35Z, first full v5+v6+v7 pass). 0081 token still\nlive on Nacho. 0085 awaits his ruling. The twin DIRECTION watch is\narmed: if anyone heals the wrong direction again despite the printed\ncontract, the guard itself failed."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T12:19:07.97575123Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T12:19:08.088304196Z","message...[+217 chars]
